text:
From: Nazar AlQuraishi Sent: Wednesday, November 11, 2020 5:40 PM To: Salem Ajluni [email redacted] Cc: Ethnic Studies <EthnicStudies@cde.ca.gov> Subject: [EXTERNAL] Re: A Letter to the California Department of Education RE: Ethnic Studies: Arab American Lesson Plan

I second the petition from Mr. Ajluni regarding the Arab American Studies and the other concerns that he had. Please accept my best regards

Nazar AlQuraishi San Jose 95123

To Whom It May Concern: I am writing in support of the Save Arab American Studies Coalition and to request that you:

1. Include the Arab American lesson plan submitted to the California Depart of Education by members of the original Ethnic Studies Model Curriculum (ESMC) advisory committee;

2. Re-insert Arab American studies to its rightful place within Asian American studies;

3. Align all lesson plans to the guiding principles of ethnic studies, which are anti-racist, decolonial and liberatory;

4. Reject debunked definitions of antisemitism contained in the curriculum or sample lesson plans that equate criticism of Israel with antisemitism. Simply put, antisemitism is discrimination against, violence towards, or stereotypes of Jews for being Jewish.

Sincerely,

Salem Ajluni San Jose, CA 95128

From: yousef_salem Sent: Friday, November 13, 2020 9:35 AM To: Ethnic Studies; Superintendent; Subject:

To Whom It May Concern: I am writing in support of the Save Arab American Studies Coalition and to request that you:

1. Include the Arab American lesson plan submitted to the California Depart of Education by members of the original Ethnic Studies Model Curriculum (ESMC) advisory committee;

2. Re-insert Arab American studies to its rightful place within Asian American studies;

3. Align all lesson plans to the guiding principles of ethnic studies, which are anti-racist, decolonial and liberatory;

4. Reject debunked definitions of antisemitism contained in the curriculum or sample lesson plans that equate criticism of Israel with antisemitism. Simply put, antisemitism is discrimination against, violence towards, or stereotypes of Jews qua Jewish. Arabs are also Semites.

Sincerely,

Yousef Salem

From: Salem Ajluni Sent: Wednesday, November 11, 2020 5:01 PM To: Ethnic Studies <EthnicStudies@cde.ca.gov> Subject: [EXTERNAL] A Letter to the California Department of Education RE: Ethnic Studies: Arab American Lesson Plan

To Whom It May Concern: I am writing in support of the Save Arab American Studies Coalition and to request that you:

1. Include the Arab American lesson plan submitted to the California Depart of Education by members of the original Ethnic Studies Model Curriculum (ESMC) advisory committee;

2. Re-insert Arab American studies to its rightful place within Asian American studies;

3. Align all lesson plans to the guiding principles of ethnic studies, which are anti-racist, decolonial and liberatory;

4. Reject debunked definitions of antisemitism contained in the curriculum or sample lesson plans that equate criticism of Israel with antisemitism. Simply put, antisemitism is discrimination against, violence towards, or stereotypes of Jews for being Jewish.

Sincerely,

Salem Ajluni San Jose, CA 95128

To the Cal. Dept. of Education / Instructional Quality Commission and Those This May Concern: I am a California resident with a Master's in Public Administration;

The proposed curriculum (ESMC), as it stands, is deeply disturbing. As a Jewish California, I am shocked by the anti-Semitic language, couched as anti-Israel rhetoric. The Department of Education should reconsider a course that promotes hatred and bias in our school system, especially since it targets children who do not have the knowledge to understand the bias. The Jews of California should not be targeted in a way that would be unthinkable for other ethnicities. As someone who has a background in Public Administration, I know that this is something that would sincerely damage our school system for years to come. This is a choice that could demonstrate that being antiSemitic is a good option.

I appreciate your consideration. I encourage you to contact me if there is anything I can clarify. Best regards, Isaac H. Winer, Esq. California resident for 34 years Parent of public high school student
